  data:  # 47c1
    kind: work_item  # 47c1
    title: "ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite FAILS at dev 45280d90 and no earlier commit has a full-suite context at all — likely first run of a workflow its own header calls DORMANT/unvalidated; ci-debug cannot fetch the cron job's log"  # 47c1
    status: todo_hard  # 47c1
    priority: 1  # 47c1
    parent:  # 47c1
    tags:  # 47c1
    - ci  # 47c1
    - test_infra  # 47c1
    - trunk_green  # 47c1
    isbefore: []  # 47c1
    duplicate_of: []  # 47c1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 47c1
    pr_ref:  # 47c1
    description: "OBSERVED 2026-08-11. `ci-debug runs` reports, at dev 45280d90:\n\n    success   ci/woodpecker/push/woodpecker   @ 45280d90\n    FAILURE   ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite   @ 45280d90\n\n45280d90 is the INV-safig merge. Filing rather than fixing because the cause is\nnot yet identified and the evidence points away from that merge.\n\nWHY I DO NOT BELIEVE THE MERGE CAUSED IT — stated as evidence, not as a defence:\n  - smart-test --full on that tree: 22,419 passed, 6 skipped, 17 xfailed, exit 0.\n  - the top-level governance suite, which full-suite runs separately and which\n    smart-test --full does NOT include: `pytest tests/ -o addopts=\"\"` -> 1,531\n    passed, exit 0.\n  - check-package-coverage: all 6 packages 100% in isolation, exit 0.\n  - mypy strict ratchet: 672, baseline 672, delta +0.\nThat covers every suite full-suite is documented to run.\n\nTHE STRONGER HYPOTHESIS is that this job has never successfully run.\n`.woodpecker/full-suite.yml`'s own header states it \"Lands DORMANT (INERT on\nCodeberg/Forgejo, which reads .github/workflows/**, not .woodpecker/);\nvalidated/iterated at cutover.\" The cutover to GitHub + Woodpecker has since\nhappened, so a first real execution of an unvalidated workflow is the likeliest\nexplanation. Corroborating: no earlier commit carries a full-suite context at all\n(checked 0ca355ed, 9e5487ff, 75af0817, 1102e75b, 3f00cc35, 63059803 — none), so\nthere is no green baseline to regress FROM. That absence is also why \"it was\nalready broken\" cannot be asserted yet either.\n\nBLOCKED ON LOG ACCESS, which is the first thing to fix. `ci-debug logs\n<job> <sha>` returns the PUSH job's output for every job name tried\n(\"full-suite\", \"cron/full-suite\", \"ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite\", \"test-core\",\n\"test-mainstream\", \"self-validate\") — all five produced the identical\n\"3 selected files ... 396 passed\" transcript, which is the per-PR pipeline, not\nthe cron one. So either ci-debug resolves only the most recent pipeline for a\nSHA, or cron pipelines are addressed differently. Until that is resolved the\nactual failure message has not been seen by anyone, and NOTHING here should be\ntreated as a diagnosis.\n\nNEXT STEPS, in order:\n  1. Make `ci-debug logs` able to address a cron pipeline (or find the existing\n     way). Without it this item cannot progress.\n  2. Read the actual failure. Only then decide revert-vs-fix.\n  3. If it is a first-run infrastructure failure, note that full-suite has been\n     REPORTING nothing rather than PASSING, and price what that means: the\n     whole-package isolation gate the project believes runs twice daily may not\n     have run at all since cutover. That is the finding that would matter most,\n     and it bears directly on WI-kahar (whether the local pkgcov gate can be\n     relocated) — if the cron gate is dead, the local one is currently the ONLY\n     whole-package isolation coverage anywhere."  # 47c1
    fields:  # 47c1
      statement: "dev must be green on every gate that reports on it, and every reporting gate must have a readable log."  # 47c1
- op: discuss  # bdcd
  at: '2026-08-14T01:13:18Z'  # bdcd
  by: agent  # bdcd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bdcd
  clock: 2  # bdcd
  nonce: bdcd  # bdcd
  message: "LOG ACCESS FIXED — the item's own stated first step. dev 87f567d202.\n\nROOT CAUSE. Several gates report on one commit and each is a SEPARATE\nWoodpecker pipeline with its own target_url. _github_fetch_job_log took the\nFIRST status carrying one and broke; the job name was applied only later, to\npick a STEP inside a pipeline already chosen wrongly. The job name was\nanswering the wrong question — it selected within a pipeline, and nothing\nselected the pipeline. That is why five different job names all returned the\nidentical push transcript.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE MERGED TREE, both arms, which is what makes it a control\nrather than one lucky call:\n  ci-debug logs cron/full-suite 45280d90  -> cron log, \"3 failed, 1498 passed\"\n  ci-debug logs push/woodpecker 45280d90  -> push log, \"396 passed\"\nA no-name invocation now prefers the gate that FAILED (it used to hand back\nthe GREEN pipeline while another gate was red), mirroring the step-level rule\nalready in the file.\n\nWHAT THE LOG SAYS. Three failures, none a product regression, consistent with\nthis item's evidence that the tree is green locally:\n  tests/test_rct_public_api_pinned.py x2   ModuleNotFoundError: 'rich'\n  tests/test_smart_test_mypy_gate.py  x1   rc=2 (mypy infra failure) where the\n                                           fixture expects rc=1 (regression)\nRead off the same log, the step's pip line installs pytest, pytest-xdist,\npyyaml and hypothesis and nothing else — no hypergumbo package, no rich, no\nmypy.\n\nSTAYING OPEN. This item's statement has TWO clauses: dev green on every\nreporting gate, AND every reporting gate readable. Only the second is\nsatisfied. Closing now would be a proxy closure of exactly the INV-nufob kind.\n\nA DISCREPANCY I COULD NOT EXPLAIN, recorded rather than smoothed over,\nbecause the next person will hit it. Reconstructing that container locally (a\nclean venv with exactly those four installs) fails 8 of the 9 tests in\ntest_rct_public_api_pinned.py with \"No module named 'hypergumbo_core'\". CI\nfailed only 2, and on a DIFFERENT missing module. The file is byte-identical\nat that SHA (git show: same 9 tests) and the workflow file is byte-identical\ntoo. So the CI container had hypergumbo_core importable and rich absent,\nwhich neither my reconstruction nor the workflow explains. I did not change\nthe install list on top of a model that is demonstrably wrong — that would be\nguessing with a green tick on it. Filed as INV-* below for diagnosis USING\nthe tool this PR delivers.\n\nGOVERNANCE OBSERVATION FOR THE OWNER, not acted on. scripts/lib/github-api.sh\nis NOT in CODEOWNERS; only scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh is. The governance gate\nprotects the DORMANT backend and leaves the LIVE one open. The fix landed in\nthe unprotected file because the list is explicit and it is not on it, but\nthe asymmetry looks like an oversight from the Forgejo->GitHub cutover rather\nthan a decision."  # bdcd
- op: discuss  # afef
  at: '2026-08-15T04:44:29Z'  # afef
  by: agent  # afef
  actor: jgstern_agent  # afef
  clock: 3  # afef
  nonce: afef  # afef
  message: "A SECOND DOOR IN THE SAME WALL, found by this item's own fix being used.\nPR #348.\n\nThe WI-zavut fix answered \"several gates on one commit - which pipeline?\".\nIt did not answer \"the named gate is not on this commit at all\". Asking for\ncron/full-suite on dev ea0d6a83ab (which carries ONLY push/woodpecker)\nmatched no status, fell past the prefer-the-failed-gate rule because nothing\nhad failed, landed on statuses[0], and printed the PUSH pipeline's GREEN\ntranscript at rc 0 with nothing marking the substitution.\n\n  ci-debug logs cron/full-suite ea0d6a83ab  ->  \"1556 passed\"  rc 0\n\nI nearly filed that as \"the cron gate is green now\". What stopped it was the\nshape of the number, not the tool: 1556 tests in 16.5s off an 85-file\nmanifest is the push gate, and full-suite is 22k tests. This file's own rule\n- a clean extreme is usually the instrument - fired on the instrument built\nto satisfy this very item.\n\nFIXED: both selectors (pipeline and step) now report HOW they chose, and both\nroute through one _wp_warn_substituted rather than growing a second copy of\nthe rule. The warning is deliberately narrow. ONE name is tried as a gate and\nthen as a step, so a fallback at either level ALONE is ordinary - a gate name\nnever matches a step, a step name never matches a gate - and warning on the\ncommon path would train the reader to skip it. Only when NEITHER matches has\nanything been substituted. The two quiet cases are pinned by their own tests;\nWI-zavut's five pipeline-selection tests are untouched and still pass.\n\nCORRECTION TO THIS ITEM'S RECORD: \"no earlier commit has a full-suite context\nat all\" no longer holds. The cron IS firing on schedule (0 1,13 UTC) and its\ntwo most recent runs are 7812c0b6a0 (08-14 13:00 UTC) and 1de346d6d4 (08-15\n01:00 UTC), both FAILURE. The reason the dev TIP has no such context is\nsimply that no cron has fired since it was pushed - which is also why the\nabsent-gate path was reachable at all.\n\nSTILL OPEN, unchanged in kind: clause 2 (log access) was already satisfied\nand is now more honest; clause 1 (dev green on every reporting gate) awaits\nthe 13:00 UTC cron, with the three known failures fixed under INV-vazuh in\nPR #349."  # afef
- op: discuss  # 33ee
